From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tsuji Riya <tsuji.riya@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 03:14:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 21/28] drm/tiny: Add Celestial CNC1800L display driver

Output 0 and graphics layer 1 of the display feeder and the Silicon
Image HDMI transmitter of the SoC: modes from the EDID read through
the transmitter DDC master (exposed as an I2C adapter), pixel clock
from the display PLL, RGB565, XRGB8888 and ARGB8888, an AVI InfoFrame
from the mode (YCbCr 4:4:4 as the DF converts, BT.709 for HD, BT.601
for SD), and RGB without InfoFrames for DVI sinks. The transmitter
registers are the ones the vendor hdmi.bin writes, recorded in an
emulator; SYS_CTRL1 must keep HEN/VEN (0x37) and the DF graphics format
AlphaIsValid for RGB565, or the TV gets no signal.

The transmitter sends the blank colour instead of the picture unless
HDCP encryption is on, so do the HDCP 1.x authentication with the
keys held in the transmitter: BKSV over DDC, An and Aksv
to the receiver, R0 compared, then ENC_EN under AVMUTE; no repeater
part 2. The receiver does not answer on DDC for a while after the link
comes up, so this runs from a work every second, which also polls HPD
for hotplug (the HPD change interrupt bit is set again by the set-up
itself) and compares Ri every 2s, since a TV switching inputs drops
HDCP without an HPD change.

The CEC controller (Silicon Image CPI at +0x1000 of the transmitter) is
a CEC adapter, polled every 20ms; its registers hang the bus with the
CEC clock off.

1080p60/50, 720p60, 480p, 1024x768, RGB565 and XRGB8888 show the right
colour bars on hardware; CEC transmits but is not tested against a CEC
sink.

Register offsets and sequences come from the vendor GPL kernel sources
and from a trace of the vendor hdmi.bin; no vendor code is reused.

Signed-off-by: Tsuji Riya <tsuji.riya@gmail.com>
